# writeback — a write is proven by reading it back from Home Assistant
# directly, never through hactl (INVARIANTS.md H-12).
#
# The surface is the live cobra tree: every command carrying a --confirm flag,
# walked once for both this ledger and confirm.manifest and re-keyed by
# TestWriteBackSurfaceIsClosed. H-2 makes --confirm the definition of a mutating
# command, so both laws quantify over one set and it is derived once. H-12 states
# its rule as "every write family" and enforces it by citing the families that
# had a round-trip test written for them; the tree carried thirty-four commands
# on the day this ledger was written, and the gate, not this sentence, counts.
#
# `proven` is claimed only where a named test meets H-12 whole. Four clauses:
#
#   1. it drives the command with --confirm;
#   2. it reads the result out of Home Assistant itself — /api/states, the WS
#      entity/label/area registries, lovelace/config, the config-entry list, the
#      automation config API — and not back through hactl;
#   3. it compares the whole document, so a write that dropped or added a key it
#      was never asked to touch fails;
#   4. it asserts at least one field the command never prints, as an independent
#      witness that the document reached HA whole and not merely in name.
#
# Clauses 3 and 4 are stated for a write that leaves a document behind. A DELETE
# leaves none: there is nothing to compare and no field to witness. They are not
# waived for deletes — read literally that would condemn every delete in the
# ledger, and this file would be inconsistent with itself. They land on the only
# document a delete has, the one that must survive it:
#
#   3'. THE DRY RUN CHANGED NOTHING, checked independently. The command is run
#       once without --confirm and the target must still be there afterwards —
#       still listed, still stateful, still itself. This is the delete's
#       whole-document compare: the document held to being unchanged is the one
#       still standing.
#   4'. THE ABSENCE IS COMPLETE, AND NOTHING ELSE WENT WITH IT. After --confirm,
#       HA is read for the absence, and for an entity-producing family that means
#       both halves — no registry entry AND no state (assertNoGhost, retried),
#       because HA leaves a `restored` ghost behind and a gone-from-the-file check
#       would pass straight over one. Where the definition shares a file with a
#       seeded neighbour, the neighbour is read back too. That is a delete's
#       independent witness: "a field the command never prints" has no
#       delete-shaped analogue, but "a thing the command was never asked to touch"
#       does.
#
# This is what separates the five deletes claimed `proven` here — dash, config,
# script, tpl, helper — from `hactl auto delete`, which is debt, and it is worth
# spelling out because clauses 3 and 4 read literally do not separate them at all.
# All five run the dry run first and read the target back to prove it was inert
# (from HA for four of them; through the companion, off the config file, for `tpl
# delete`, whose definition HA exposes no API for). All five read HA for the
# absence, the three entity-producing ones through assertNoGhost. The two that
# share a YAML file with a seeded neighbour, `script delete` and `tpl delete`,
# assert that neighbour still parses and still holds its own document.
# TestE2EAutoDeleteByAliasCLI does none of it: it seeds, deletes with --confirm,
# and polls the entity registry for the absence. That is clause 2 and nothing
# else.
#
# Reading back through `hactl … ls`, `dash show --raw` or `ref scan` proves hactl
# consistent with itself and is recorded as debt, which is what the law's second
# paragraph demands. So is reading HA for one clause and stopping there: a
# `proven` that means "cleared the easiest clause" makes the ledger decoration,
# and a ledger nobody can trust is worse than no ledger.
#
# Blind spot, stated rather than assumed: a command that mutates HA *without* a
# --confirm gate appears on neither this surface nor confirm's. It is an H-2
# defect first, and nothing yet derives "every mutating command carries
# --confirm".
#
# The ceiling is the number of write commands nothing reads back from HA. It may
# fall without ceremony and may only rise by editing this line on purpose.
#
# Raised 10 -> 11 on purpose: `hactl auto delete` was claimed `proven` on a test
# that reads HA — clause 2 — and does nothing else H-12 asks of a delete: no
# inert dry run, no complete absence, no untouched neighbour. A `proven` that does
# not mean "the law is met" turns the ledger into decoration, which is the failure
# mode the ledger exists to prevent, so the entry moved to debt and the ceiling
# moved with it. The bar above was sharpened in the same pass, because as first
# written it condemned the other five deletes alongside it and named no
# discriminator; the demotion is right, the reasoning it shipped with was not.
#ceiling 11

# --- proven: a named test writes with --confirm and reads HA back -------------
#
# auto apply / auto rollback: reads GetAutomationConfig from HA's REST API before
# and after, asserts the trigger (a field the command never prints) as the
# witness, and compares the whole canonicalised document after the rollback.
hactl auto apply = proven: TestAutoApplyRollbackRoundTrip
hactl auto rollback = proven: TestAutoApplyRollbackRoundTrip
#
# script create/apply/delete + tpl create/delete + helper create/delete: drive
# the real binary and read HA's own /api/states for the entity, with `icon` /
# `unit_of_measurement` as witnesses the CLI never prints; the delete half reads
# the WS entity registry and requires no ghost. The stored document is compared
# whole through the companion as a second, independent read.
hactl script create = proven: TestE2EScriptWriteRoundTripCLI
hactl script apply = proven: TestE2EScriptWriteRoundTripCLI
hactl script delete = proven: TestE2EScriptWriteRoundTripCLI
hactl tpl create = proven: TestE2ETplWriteRoundTripCLI
hactl tpl delete = proven: TestE2ETplWriteRoundTripCLI
hactl helper create = proven: TestE2EHelperWriteRoundTripCLI
hactl helper delete = proven: TestE2EHelperWriteRoundTripCLI
#
# dash create/save/delete + replace: read the stored config straight from HA's
# lovelace/config and the registration from lovelace/dashboards/list, compare the
# whole document, and assert the dry run left it byte-identical. `icon` and
# `mode` are witnesses the command never echoes.
hactl dash create = proven: TestDashCreateSaveDeleteRoundTrip
hactl dash save = proven: TestDashCreateSaveDeleteRoundTrip
hactl dash delete = proven: TestDashCreateSaveDeleteRoundTrip
hactl dash replace = proven: TestDashReplaceRoundTrip
#
# ent set-label / set-area: read the entity's registry entry over WS before and
# after, and compare the whole entry with the changed field blanked, so a write
# that also touched a field it was never asked to touch fails the witness.
hactl ent set-label = proven: TestEntSetLabelRoundTrip
hactl ent set-area = proven: TestEntSetAreaRoundTrip
#
# device set-area / set-label: same shape one registry over — read the device's
# registry entry over WS before and after, whole-entry compare with the changed
# field blanked (manufacturer/model/sw_version as never-printed witnesses),
# dry-run-changed-nothing checked independently, and HA's own area_entities
# asked whether the device's entities inherited the area (H-8).
hactl device set-area = proven: TestDeviceSetAreaLabelRoundTrip
hactl device set-label = proven: TestDeviceSetAreaLabelRoundTrip
#
# ent rename (two writes, both read back from their own oracles): the registry
# half from HA's WS entity registry (new id present with the old unique_id as
# witness, old id absent), the reference half from HA's automation config API
# — never through hactl. Dry-run-changed-nothing checked on both halves.
# Runs in make test-companion: the reference half needs the companion.
hactl ent rename = proven: TestE2EEntRenameCLI
#
# config flow-start/flow-step/delete: read HA's own config-entry list over REST
# after each step, with the entry's title and state as witnesses. flow-start's
# own write is a server-side flow, witnessed transitively — a stubbed
# StartConfigFlowOnce hands out a flow id that flow-step cannot complete, so no
# entry appears in HA's list.
hactl config flow-start = proven: TestConfigFlowCreateDeleteRoundTrip
hactl config flow-step = proven: TestConfigFlowCreateDeleteRoundTrip
hactl config delete = proven: TestConfigFlowCreateDeleteRoundTrip
#
# config options: reads the new option value back out of a fresh HA options flow's
# seeded form default, asserts the dry run changed nothing, and asserts the
# restore.
hactl config options = proven: TestConfigOptionsRoundTrip

# --- exempt: HA holds no record to read back ---------------------------------
hactl companion wireguard config = exempt: writes a .conf onto the add-on's /data through the companion; the tunnel is not HA state and HA's API holds no record of it to read back
hactl companion wireguard up = exempt: starts an interface inside the add-on container; the only oracle is the companion's own wireguard status, and HA never learns of it
hactl companion wireguard down = exempt: stops that interface; same absence of any HA-side record, so there is no HA read-back this rule could ask for
hactl rollback = exempt: deprecated alias that dispatches to the same runRollback and the same --confirm flag variable as auto rollback, whose write TestAutoApplyRollbackRoundTrip reads back from HA; there is one write path, not two

# --- debt: the rule applies and the read-back does not meet it ---------------
hactl auto create = debt: TestE2EAutoCreateCLI asserts hactl's own "created automation" and the entity_id line it prints from the reload confirmation; nothing reads the new automation out of HA's states or its config API, and no witness field is checked
hactl auto delete = debt: TestE2EAutoDeleteByAliasCLI does read HA's entity registry over WebSocket and requires the entry to be gone, so it clears the "read HA, not hactl" bar — but that is one clause of H-12 and it meets no other: no whole-document compare, no assertion on a field the command never prints, no "the dry run changed nothing" read-back, and the write's actual target, the removal of the definition from automations.yaml, is read back through the companion (TestE2EAutoDeleteByObjectIDCLI) rather than from HA's own automation config API
hactl area create = debt: TestAreaCreateDelete verifies the write by reading it back through `hactl area ls` — hactl proving hactl — instead of HA's WS area registry, and asserts no field the command never prints
hactl area delete = debt: same read-back-through-hactl gap: the delete is checked with `hactl area ls`, and HA's own area registry is never consulted for the absence
hactl floor create = debt: TestFloorCreateDelete reads the result back through `hactl floor ls`; HA's WS floor registry, the only independent oracle, is never asked, and level/icon are unwitnessed
hactl floor delete = debt: the delete asserts only hactl's "deleted floor" echo; nothing reads HA's floor registry to see whether the floor is really gone
hactl label create = debt: TestLabelCreate reads it back through `hactl label ls`; TestWebSocketLabelCreateAndList does consult HA but drives the haapi client, not the CLI, so the command's own write is unproven
hactl label delete = debt: the delete is verified with `hactl label ls` again, and no test reads HA's label registry back or checks that detached entities lost the label
hactl svc call = debt: every svc call test asserts hactl's "called <service>" echo, which is printed the moment the call returns nil; nothing reads the effect (a state, a persistent notification) back out of HA
hactl script run = debt: the run tests assert "executed script.<id>"; HA records last_triggered and the entities the sequence touches, and no test reads either back to prove the script really ran
hactl ref replace = debt: TestE2ERefReplaceCLI confirms the moved literal with `ref scan`, the same path that wrote it; the rewritten automation is readable from HA's own config API and nothing reads it there
